/-!
# The derived completion of a coherent module is the limit of its adic quotients, Tag 0A0K (3)

Let `Y` be a quasi-compact locally Noetherian scheme, let `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪_Y` be a quasi-coherent sheaf of
ideals generated by global sections `f_1, …, f_r`, and let `ℱ` be a coherent `𝒪_Y`-module. Then

`ℱ^∧ = lim ℱ/ℐⁿℱ`,

the derived completion of `ℱ` along `ℐ` being isomorphic in `D(𝒪_Y)` to the **ordinary** inverse
limit of the adic quotients, placed in degree zero. This is part (3) of [Stacks, Tag 0A0K].

## How the three inputs fit together

* [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] presents `ℱ^∧` as a derived limit of the Koszul tower
  `n ↦ ℱ ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 (K^n)^∨`, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.koszulTensorDualSystemCoefficientIso` identifies that tower with
  the `r`-fold shift of the dual Koszul tower with coefficients. So `ℱ^∧` is a derived limit of
  `(koszulDualSystemOn ⋙ ⟦r⟧) ⋙ Q`.
* Part (4) of the tag presents `lim ℱ/ℐⁿℱ`, placed in degree zero, as a derived limit of the adic
  quotient tower.
* The two towers are **pro-isomorphic**, which is
  `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulAdicProIso`: the dual Koszul tower is pro-isomorphic to the tower of
  its top cohomology sheaves by [Stacks, Tag 0G3D], those are the quotients by the powers of the
  generators, and those are pro-isomorphic to the adic quotients by the pigeonhole containments
  `ℐ^{rn+1} ⊆ (f_1ⁿ, …, f_rⁿ) ⊆ ℐⁿ`.

[Stacks, Tag 0H9L] then says that two derived limits of pro-isomorphic towers are isomorphic, and
that isomorphism is the assertion. The shift by `r` is absorbed at the end: `Q` commutes with the
shift, and the shift by `r` of the single functor in degree `r` is the single functor in degree
zero, `CategoryTheory.SingleFunctors.shiftIso`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulAdicProIso` — the shifted dual Koszul tower and the adic quotient
  tower in degree zero are isomorphic as pro-objects of `D(𝒪_Y)`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient` and
  `Stacks.Algebraization.hasEventualInverses_koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient` — the same
  comparison **as a genuine natural transformation of towers with eventual inverses**, which is
  what `NoetherianAdicCompletion.IsDerivedLimitWithProjections.comp_of_hasEventualInverses`
  consumes and `InverseSequence.ProIso` cannot supply.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedLimit_koszulDualTower_completion` — [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] read on
  the shifted dual Koszul tower.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.nonempty_iso_completion_limit_adicQuotientSystem` — **part (3) of
  [Stacks, Tag 0A0K]**.

## The hypotheses, and what they are for

`[CompactSpace Y]` is genuine and is used in exactly one place: the Artin–Rees level that makes the
Koszul cohomology sheaves pro-zero below the top degree is produced one per member of an affine
cover, and the proof takes the maximum of finitely many of them
(`Stacks.KoszulProZero.exists_koszulPowerComplexOnDown_homologyMap_eq_zero_of_coherent`). With
infinitely many charts those levels need not be bounded. `[IsLocallyNoetherian Y]` and
`[ℱ.IsCoherent]` are the tag's own hypotheses, and are used for the same Artin–Rees input.

The source states part (3) for an arbitrary quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals, and the statement here
asks that `ℐ` be generated by the global sections `f_1, …, f_r`. That is the scope in which the
Koszul tower of the formula exists on `X` itself: the completion functor used below is the Čech
model `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.completionFunctor` of [Stacks, Tag 099B] at `f_1, …, f_r`,
and the derived limit it is computed by is the tower on the powers of those sections.

The *object* `ℱ^∧` is not restricted to that scope. The reflector onto the derived-complete
objects of `D(𝒪)` exists along any sheaf of ideals of finite type — [Stacks, Tag 099F] as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` — and a quasi-coherent sheaf of
ideals of a locally Noetherian scheme is of finite type, by
`Stacks.Algebraization.isFiniteType_idealSheafSiteSubmodule` of
`Completion/IdealSheafSiteFiniteType.lean`. What is restricted is the *route*: [Stacks, Tag 0A0E]
at a finite-type ideal sheaf computes `ℱ^∧` by a Koszul tower only after restriction to a slice
over an object carrying a finite generating family, and the comparison this file produces is
non-canonical, so the per-slice comparisons cannot be glued. The module documentation of
`Completion/AdicQuotientDerivedLimit.lean` records what a proof at the tag's own hypotheses
would take.

There *is* a canonical comparison `ℱ^∧ ⟶ (lim ℱ/ℐⁿℱ)[0]` to be invertible, at the tag's own
hypotheses:
`Stacks.Algebraization.existsUnique_comparison_derivedCompletion_limit_adicQuotientSystem` of
`Completion/AdicQuotientLimitComplete.lean`, built from the derived completeness of the target
(`Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedComplete_single_limit_adicQuotientSystem`, with neither
quasi-compactness nor global generation) and the universal property of the completion unit. What
the statement below contributes that the canonical comparison does not is the isomorphism itself;
what it costs is `[CompactSpace Y]` and the global generating family.

## Implementation notes

The derived category is taken with `CategoryTheory.HasDerivedCategory.standard`, which is the
instance [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] is stated with; the tag's part (4) is stated with the local instance
`Stacks.Algebraization.modulesHasDerivedCategory`, which unfolds to the same thing, so the two
compose. Enabling the other one instead makes the instance search for the shift's additivity and
for the countable coproduct of the Koszul tower fail at the site's spelling of the category of
modules, even though both are derivable there — the two spellings are definitionally but not
reducibly equal.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0K)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G3D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G3D)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0H9L](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H9L)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

universe u

attribute [local instance] AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.submoduleCompleteLattice

namespace Stacks.Algebraization

attribute [local instance] modulesEnoughInjectives HasDerivedCategory.standard

section IdealSheaf

variable (Y : Scheme.{u}) {r : ℕ}
  (f : Fin r → Presheaf.globalSections
    ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf).O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))

/-- **The quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals generated by the chosen global sections**
`f_1, …, f_r ∈ Γ(Y, 𝒪_Y)`: `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop` of the ideal of
global functions they span. -/
def koszulIdealSheafData : Y.IdealSheafData :=
  Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span (Set.range (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f)))

/-- **The affine sections of that ideal sheaf are spanned by the restrictions of the
generators.** This is the generation hypothesis the pro-isomorphism of
`Stacks.Algebraization.proIso_koszulTopQuotientSystem_adicQuotientSystem` asks for, discharged
rather than assumed: `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop` extends an ideal of
global functions by taking, on each affine open, the ideal generated by its restrictions, and
the image of a span is the span of the image. -/
theorem koszulIdealSheafData_ideal (U : Y.affineOpens) :
    (koszulIdealSheafData Y f).ideal U =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun i => globalRes U (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f i)) := by
  rw [koszulIdealSheafData, Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop_ideal, Ideal.map_span,
    ← Set.range_comp]
  rfl

/-- **Every quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals generated on each affine open by the restrictions of
`f_1, …, f_r` is `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulIdealSheafData`**: an `IdealSheafData` is determined
by its affine components. So a statement about that one ideal sheaf is a statement about every
ideal sheaf satisfying the generation condition, which is why the condition can be a definition
below rather than a hypothesis. -/
theorem eq_koszulIdealSheafData (I : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (hgen : ∀ U : Y.affineOpens, I.ideal U =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun i => globalRes U (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f i))) :
    I = koszulIdealSheafData Y f :=
  Scheme.IdealSheafData.ext
    (funext fun U => (hgen U).trans (koszulIdealSheafData_ideal Y f U).symm)

end IdealSheaf

section Main

open scoped IsMulCommutative


variable (Y : Scheme.{u}) [IsLocallyNoetherian Y] [CompactSpace Y] {r : ℕ}
  (f : Fin r → Presheaf.globalSections
    ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf).O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))
  (ℱ : Y.Modules) [ℱ.IsCoherent]

/-- **The shifted dual Koszul tower, read in the derived category.** This is the tower of the
Koszul formula of [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] at `ℱ`, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.koszulTensorDualSystemCoefficientIso`. -/
def koszulShiftedDualDerivedTower : ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ DerivedCategory Y.Modules :=
  (koszulDualTowerOn Y f ℱ ⋙ shiftFunctor (CochainComplex Y.Modules ℤ) (r : ℤ)) ⋙
    DerivedCategory.Q

/-- **The shifted dual Koszul tower and the adic quotient tower in degree zero are isomorphic as
pro-objects of `D(𝒪_Y)`.**

Three pro-isomorphisms are composed: the comparison of the dual Koszul tower with the tower of its
top cohomology sheaves ([Stacks, Tag 0G3D]), the comparison of those quotients with the adic
quotients (the pigeonhole containments), and two identifications of functors — `Q` commutes with
the shift, and the `r`-fold shift of the single functor in degree `r` is the single functor in
degree zero. -/
def koszulAdicProIso (I : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (hgen : ∀ U : Y.affineOpens, I.ideal U =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun i => globalRes U (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f i))) :
    InverseSequence.ProIso (koszulShiftedDualDerivedTower Y f ℱ)
      (adicQuotientSystem I ℱ ⋙ DerivedCategory.singleFunctor Y.Modules 0) :=
  CategoryTheory.Pro.proIsoTrans
    (CategoryTheory.Pro.proIsoTrans
      (InverseSequence.ProIso.ofNatIso (Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (koszulDualTowerOn Y f ℱ)
        (DerivedCategory.Q.commShiftIso (r : ℤ))))
      (InverseSequence.ProIso.whiskerRight
        (CategoryTheory.Pro.proIsoTrans
          (proIso_koszulDualTower_koszulTopQuotientSystem Y f ℱ)
          (InverseSequence.ProIso.whiskerRight
            (proIso_koszulTopQuotientSystem_adicQuotientSystem Y f ℱ I hgen)
            (HomologicalComplex.single Y.Modules (ComplexShape.up ℤ) ((r : ℤ) - 1 + 1) ⋙
              DerivedCategory.Q)))
        (shiftFunctor (DerivedCategory Y.Modules) (r : ℤ))))
    (InverseSequence.ProIso.ofNatIso (Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (adicQuotientSystem I ℱ)
      ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctors Y.Modules).shiftIso (r : ℤ) 0 ((r : ℤ) - 1 + 1) (by ring))))

/-- **The comparison of the shifted dual Koszul tower with the adic quotient tower in degree
zero, as a genuine natural transformation.**

This is `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulAdicProIso` with the transformation kept: the same four
steps are composed, but the two pro-isomorphisms with content are replaced by the natural
transformations behind them — `Stacks.Algebraization.koszulDualToTopQuotientDerived` and
`Stacks.Algebraization.koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient` — and the two identifications of
functors are natural isomorphisms already.

`InverseSequence.ProIso` carries only a zig-zag at a reindexing and has no component
`X_n ⟶ Y_n` at all, so it cannot be fed to
`NoetherianAdicCompletion.IsDerivedLimitWithProjections.comp_of_hasEventualInverses`, which is
the statement that carries the projections of a derived-limit presentation along a comparison of
towers. This transformation can, by
`Stacks.Algebraization.hasEventualInverses_koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient`. -/
def koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient (I : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (hgen : ∀ U : Y.affineOpens, I.ideal U =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun i => globalRes U (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f i))) :
    koszulShiftedDualDerivedTower Y f ℱ ⟶
      adicQuotientSystem I ℱ ⋙ DerivedCategory.singleFunctor Y.Modules 0 :=
  (Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (koszulDualTowerOn Y f ℱ)
        (DerivedCategory.Q.commShiftIso (r : ℤ))).hom ≫
    Functor.whiskerRight (koszulDualToTopQuotientDerived Y f ℱ)
      (shiftFunctor (DerivedCategory Y.Modules) (r : ℤ)) ≫
    Functor.whiskerRight (Functor.whiskerRight (koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient Y f ℱ I hgen)
        (HomologicalComplex.single Y.Modules (ComplexShape.up ℤ) ((r : ℤ) - 1 + 1) ⋙
          DerivedCategory.Q))
      (shiftFunctor (DerivedCategory Y.Modules) (r : ℤ)) ≫
    (Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (adicQuotientSystem I ℱ)
      ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctors Y.Modules).shiftIso (r : ℤ) 0 ((r : ℤ) - 1 + 1)
        (by ring))).hom

/-- **The comparison of the shifted dual Koszul tower with the adic quotient tower has eventual
inverses.** The two identifications of functors are isomorphisms, so they have eventual inverses
at the same level; the comparison with the top-degree quotient tower has them by [Stacks, Tag
0G3D] (`Stacks.Algebraization.hasEventualInverses_koszulDualToTopQuotientDerived`), and the
comparison of the two quotient towers by the pigeonhole containments
(`Stacks.Algebraization.hasEventualInverses_koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient`). Eventual inverses
are stable under composition and under whiskering by a functor. -/
theorem hasEventualInverses_koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient (I : Y.IdealSheafData)
    (hgen : ∀ U : Y.affineOpens, I.ideal U =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun i => globalRes U (koszulGlobalGenerator Y f i))) :
    NoetherianAdicCompletion.HasEventualInverses
      (koszulShiftedDualToAdicQuotient Y f ℱ I hgen) :=
  (hasEventualInverses_hom_of_natIso _).comp
    (((hasEventualInverses_koszulDualToTopQuotientDerived Y f ℱ).whiskerRight _).comp
      ((((hasEventualInverses_koszulTopQuotientToAdicQuotient Y f ℱ I
        hgen).whiskerRight _).whiskerRight _).comp (hasEventualInverses_hom_of_natIso _)))

omit [IsLocallyNoetherian Y] [CompactSpace Y] [ℱ.IsCoherent] in
/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0A0E] read on the shifted dual Koszul tower**: the derived completion of `ℱ`
along the ideal sheaf generated by `f_1, …, f_r` is a derived limit of
`n ↦ Q((K(𝒪_Y, f_1^{n+1}, …, f_r^{n+1}) ⊗ ℱ)⟦r⟧)`. -/
theorem isDerivedLimit_koszulDualTower_completion :
    Pretriangulated.IsDerivedLimit (koszulShiftedDualDerivedTower Y f ℱ)
      ((RingedSite.completionFunctor (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f).obj
        (DerivedCategory.Q.obj (RingedSite.singleSheafComplex
          (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) ℱ))) := by
  have hco : HasCoproduct
      ((RingedSite.koszulPowerSystem (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f ⋙
        DerivedCategory.Q).obj) :=
    inferInstanceAs (HasCoproduct (show ℕ → DerivedCategory Y.Modules from
      (RingedSite.koszulPowerSystem (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f ⋙
        DerivedCategory.Q).obj))
  exact Pretriangulated.IsDerivedLimit.of_natIso
    (RingedSite.koszulTensorDualSystemCoefficientIso
      (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f ℱ)
    (RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDualSystem_koszulPowerSystem_completion
      (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f _)

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 0A0K, part (3).** Let `Y` be a quasi-compact locally Noetherian scheme,
let `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪_Y` be a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals generated by the global sections
`f_1, …, f_r`, and let `ℱ` be a coherent `𝒪_Y`-module. Then the derived completion `ℱ^∧` of `ℱ`
along `ℐ` is isomorphic, in `D(𝒪_Y)`, to the **ordinary** inverse limit `lim ℱ/ℐⁿℱ` of the adic
quotients, placed in degree zero.

`R lim` is a predicate rather than a functor here, and a derived limit is unique only up to a
non-canonical isomorphism, which is why the conclusion is `Nonempty` of an isomorphism — exactly as
in [Stacks, Tag 0H9L], `_root_.Rlim.Pro.nonempty_iso_of_isDerivedLimit_of_pro_isomorphism`, which
is the last step of the proof.

The ideal sheaf is not a parameter with a generation hypothesis: it is
`Stacks.Algebraization.koszulIdealSheafData`, the extension to `Y` of the ideal of global
functions spanned by `f_1, …, f_r`, whose affine components are computed by
`Stacks.Algebraization.koszulIdealSheafData_ideal`. Nothing is lost by fixing it, because an
`IdealSheafData` is determined by its affine components and so every ideal sheaf generated by
those sections equals this one, which is
`Stacks.Algebraization.eq_koszulIdealSheafData`.

`[CompactSpace Y]` is a genuine hypothesis and not an artefact: the Artin–Rees level behind the
pro-vanishing of the Koszul cohomology sheaves below the top degree is produced one per member of
an affine cover, and the proof takes the maximum over a *finite* cover. See the section "The
hypotheses, and what they are for" of this file. The source asks neither for quasi-compactness
nor for `ℐ` to be globally generated; the module documentation of
`Completion/AdicQuotientDerivedLimit.lean` records what a proof at the source's own hypotheses
would take, namely a canonical comparison, which is what this route does not produce. -/
@[stacks 0A0K "part (3), `ℱ^∧ = lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` with an ordinary limit: for a coherent module `ℱ` on \
a quasi-compact locally Noetherian scheme `Y` and the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals `𝓘` \
generated by global sections `f_1, …, f_r` of `𝒪_Y`, the derived completion `ℱ^∧` along `𝓘` is \
isomorphic in `D(𝒪_Y)` to the ordinary inverse limit of the adic quotients `ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ`, placed in \
degree zero. The ideal sheaf is the extension to `Y` of the ideal those global sections span, so \
its generation by them is a definition and not a hypothesis, and every ideal sheaf so generated \
equals it. The isomorphism is non-canonical because a derived limit is, which is the form \
[Stacks, Tag 0H9L] takes"]
theorem nonempty_iso_completion_limit_adicQuotientSystem :
    Nonempty
      ((RingedSite.completionFunctor (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) f).obj
          (DerivedCategory.Q.obj (RingedSite.singleSheafComplex
            (Stacks.SheafOfModules.opensRingedSite Y.sheaf) ℱ)) ≅
        (DerivedCategory.singleFunctor Y.Modules 0).obj
          (limit (adicQuotientSystem (koszulIdealSheafData Y f) ℱ))) := by
  obtain ⟨hprod, hlim⟩ :=
    isDerivedLimit_single_limit_powerQuotients (koszulIdealSheafData Y f) ℱ
  have := hprod
  exact _root_.Rlim.Pro.nonempty_iso_of_isDerivedLimit_of_pro_isomorphism
    (koszulShiftedDualDerivedTower Y f ℱ)
    (adicQuotientSystem (koszulIdealSheafData Y f) ℱ ⋙
      DerivedCategory.singleFunctor Y.Modules 0) _ _
    (isDerivedLimit_koszulDualTower_completion Y f ℱ) hlim
    (koszulAdicProIso Y f ℱ (koszulIdealSheafData Y f) (koszulIdealSheafData_ideal Y f))

end Main

end Stacks.Algebraization

end
